  "verbalization": "Citalopram, a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Denture stomatitis through a series of indirect mechanisms. The evidence suggests that citalopram affects multiple genes involved in cellular processes that contribute to inflammation and tissue damage, ultimately leading to the condition. Specifically, citalopram inhibits protein phosphatase 1, regulatory (inhibitor) subunit 2 pseudogene 8, which in turn is encoded by Paired box protein Pax-7. This gene product is involved in cellular regulation and may influence the inflammatory response associated with Denture stomatitis. Similarly, citalopram also affects cancer/testis antigen family 47 member A1, which is encoded by Protein broad-minded (TBC1 domain family member 32), further suggesting a role in inflammatory pathways. Additionally, citalopram's interaction with DNA topoisomerase II beta, which is encoded by Sodium/hydrogen exchanger 9B2, indicates a potential role in cellular stress and repair processes that could contribute to the development of Denture stomatitis. These findings collectively suggest that citalopram may modulate key molecular pathways that are dysregulated in Denture stomatitis, although the exact mechanism by which these interactions lead to the condition remains to be fully elucidated."
